			src={cardReaderPhoto}
			alt="The Stripe Reader M2 card reader with a test card"
			class="mx-auto rounded-lg shadow-lg w-full"
		/>
		<figcaption class="text-sm text-gray-500 mt-2 italic">
			The Stripe Reader M2, ready for a contactless test card.
		</figcaption>
	</figure>
</div>

2. **A Swift iPad app, and I don't know Swift.** I'd previously used agents to build apps that I could comfortably understand myself. This time, not so much. So I really needed to ensure I had clear requirements and tests, and that my agents could drive Xcode independently.

<figure class="my-8 mx-auto max-w-2xl">
	<ZoomableImage
		src={ipadScreenshot}
		alt="Screenshot of the Swift iPad point-of-sale app"
		class="rounded-lg shadow-lg w-full"
	/>
	<figcaption class="text-center text-sm text-gray-500 mt-2 italic">
		The Swift iPad app — checkout screen.
	</figcaption>
</figure>

3. **I wanted to achieve end to end spec-driven development.** In my [prior attempt at using SpecKit](https://fintechnick.com/projects/speckit-dev) I couldn't quite get a workflow working that generated a functional application — my agent kept getting confused and lost in fruitless iteration. I wanted to do better this time.

4. **Mobile first workflow.** Sitting down at a computer outside of work for an extended time isn't something I can do often, but I do have spare pockets of time while I'm on the go. I'd already embraced asynchronous development with [Google Jules](https://jules.google.com/), but that didn't have access to all the tools on my desktop, was sometimes slow — taking hours to do a single request — and was designed to be asynchronous, so didn't work well for workflows where I wanted to iterate with the agent. I wanted a setup as similar as possible to my desktop: same tools, same performance, just accessible on the go.

So how did it go? Well, better than I expected. The hardware worked. After a bit of custom setup I wrangled Xcode into my Linux container agent workflow. I finally got a well structured and validated spec process that even worked well with the cheaper DeepSeek V4 model compared to my prior use of Gemini Pro. And I did most of it while away from my desktop. It was also a lot of fun, and I think it showed me more clearly what building software will look like with agents.

Right. Let's dig in and build a store. Target customer: a demanding toddler.

## Hey Toy Store; Meet Stripe

A store. An iPad, a Tera Mini wireless scanner, a Stripe Reader M2, a test credit card, and toy food with printed QR codes[^1].

